    "content": "Donald Trump was eligible for the military draft during the Vietnam War but received five deferments—four for college (2-S) and one for medical reasons (1-Y/4-F). He avoided service throughout the war, with his final disqualification in 1972 stemming from a medical diagnosis of bone spurs in his heels, which he has cited as a minor issue. 1964–1968 (College Deferments): Trump registered for the draft in 1964 and received four consecutive 2-S educational deferments while attending Fordham University and the University of Pennsylvania. 1968 (Medical Deferment): Following his graduation in 1968, he was classified 1-Y (unqualified for duty except in a national emergency) after a medical exam found bone spurs, allowing him to avoid the draft. 1972 (Final Classification): In February 1972, he was reclassified 4-F (medically unqualified for military service). Draft Lottery: In 1969, a national draft lottery was held for the first time, where he was assigned a high number (356), which would have likely made him unlikely to be called even without a deferment. Reports indicate the 1968 medical exemption was secured with help from a doctor who was familiar with his father, and Trump has acknowledged not serving due to these deferments.",
